On Thursday, May 2, 2013 7:48:56 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:

> On 5/2/13 4:31 PM, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > Richard:
> > So, just to be clear, what you're doing is rewriting the likelihood function in R?
>
> I looked at the code and can verify that's what's
> going on.
>
> Richard has a point. With 14K parameters, that's
> 14K * 8 bytes or so, or about 100KB/sample.
> With 1000 samples, that's 100MB of data coming
> out.  With 10,000 samples, it's a GB.
>
> - Bob
>
> > A
> > On May 2, 2013, at 1:27 PM, Richard McElreath wrote:
> >
> >> I have a very simple working example of computing WAIC from Stan samples. I did it entirely in R, though, to avoid
> >> having to store all those likelihoods in the Stan output. I'll go ahead and attach the script, in case it dispels some
> >> confusion for someone. (The script is very didactic, using loops and redundancy to make the algorithm more
> >> transparent. It could definitely be refactored for speed.)
> >>
> >> The formula I used is the p_{WAIC 2} on page 8 of Gelman, Hwang, & Vehtari.
> >>
> >> I also have an experimental module for glmer2stan that computes WAIC. I haven't put it up on github yet, as I'm not
> >> sure I understand WAIC yet, and also I'm not sure how to deal with WAIC in zero-inflated models. But this
> >> functionality will appear before long. The strategy I've taken, again, is to do everything outside of Stan, saving on
> >> storage. I have some Stan models with 20k parameters and 14k data points, and I doubt saving the likelihoods in Stan
> >> is going to be a sane approach there.
> >>
> >>
> >> On Wednesday, May 1, 2013 4:47:13 PM UTC-7, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> >>
> >>     On 5/1/13 6:07 PM, rubi wrote:
> >>
> >>     > I'm following up on an old conversation you posted on the development mailing list, regarding saving log
> >>     likelihoods for
> >>     > WAIC.
> >>     >
> >>     >  From your post I understand that ll_y should save the log-likelihoods for each data point for each sample:
> >>     >
> >>     > generated quantities {
> >>     >       double ll_y[N,T];
