Thanks for the advice. I'm using a Windows so it isn't really possible for me to profile my model, I just thought that that line was what was slowing things done because the model had been much faster before I put it in. I may try what Marcus advises, but I will also have to perform that correction because thesd is based not just on the data sds but also lowsd and booterror which are estimated. Right?

On Tuesday, May 7, 2013 1:17:45 PM UTC-5, Marcus wrote:
> As Ben said, it's unlikely that the pow() calls are the slow parts of
> this model.  Why do you think it's that line that is slowing things
> down?
>
> I assume that N is fairly large, in which case you might be able to
> speed up this line:
>
> y ~ multi_normal(muvec, diag_matrix(thesd)*meascorr*diag_matrix(thesd));
>
> by dividing y and muvec by thesd.
>
> (y./thesd) ~ mult_normal(muvec./thesd,meascorr);
>
> If thesd was a parameter, you'd also need to do a correction to lp__:
>
> lp__ <- lp__ - sum(log(thesd))
>
> but since thesd is data, this would just be a constant and is unnecessary.
>
> Hope this helps a bit.
>
> Cheers,
> Marcus

>
>
> On Tue, May 7, 2013 at 2:05 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > On Tuesday, May 7, 2013 1:16:37 PM UTC-4, Spencer Maynes wrote:
> >>
> >> The lines
> >>
> >> for (i in 1:N) {
> >> thesd[i] <- pow(pow(sds[i],2)*booterror+pow(lowsd,2),0.5);
> >> }
> >>
> >> are really slowing down my code, and I'm wondering if there is anyway to
> >> vectorize the power function, or some other way around this.
> >
> >
> > There isn't a version of the pow() function that accepts a vector, so what
> > you have there is basically the way to do it. It might be slightly faster if
> > you would use the square() and sqrt() functions instead of the the pow(x,k)
> > function that takes an arbitrary exponent. But I doubt that is slowing you
> > down a noticeable amount. If there were a Stan version of the pow() function
> > that accepted a vector, it would compile to essentially the same thing that
> > the above loop compiles to. If you profiled
> >
> > http://cran.r-project.org/doc/manuals/R-exts.html#Profiling-compiled-code
> >
> > the model, I'm almost positive that the C++ translation of these function
> > calls
> >
> > meascorr ~ lkj_corr(2);
> > y ~ multi_normal(muvec, diag_matrix(thesd)*meascorr*diag_matrix(thesd));
> >
> > would be taking up most of the computation time.
> >
> > Ben
