From uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com  Sun May 12 22:20:08 2013
From: uda33143a8db60ce8 at groups.google.com (Andrew Gelman)
Date: Sun, 12 May 2013 22:20:08 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] workaround for cut() function?
Message-ID: <ruxPAhBPI4A.SrsbM-rese0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <ruxPAhBPI4A.1xiQVN8VOkQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <ruxPAhBPI4A.Aj-BvWGOlEQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <ruxPAhBPI4A.1xiQVN8VOkQJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/ruxPAhBPI4A/m/SrsbM-rese0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi.  The reason why I (and others) perceive "cut" as bad news is because when you do "cut," you are no longer doing Bayesian inference.  To put it another way, if it's really true that you don't want inference going from A to B, I don't think you want it going from B to A either.  Joint distributions go both ways.
A

